1
00:00:05,620 --> 00:00:10,700
In this module, we will cover evaluation of the lung fields on chest radiography.

2
00:00:10,700 --> 00:00:16,700
This will conclude our discussion for an approach to interpretation of chest radiographs.

3
00:00:17,620 --> 00:00:22,400
Following completion of this module, the learner should be able to employ a systematic approach

4
00:00:22,400 --> 00:00:29,480
for interpreting chest radiographs, contrast the radiographic appearance of emphysema and

5
00:00:29,480 --> 00:00:37,760
fibrotic lung disease, identify airspace, disease, interstitial opacities, atelectasis, and pulmonary

6
00:00:37,760 --> 00:00:44,500
nodules on chest radiographs, describe and identify the silhouette and spine signs, and

7
00:00:44,500 --> 00:00:48,380
finally, distinguish pulmonary nodules, masses, and cavities.

8
00:00:49,540 --> 00:00:55,560
Just as a reminder of the big picture, this module will deal with evaluation of the lung

9
00:00:55,560 --> 00:01:03,240
fields, but remember this is in the larger context of a systematic search pattern in assessing

10
00:01:03,240 --> 00:01:04,780
a chest radiograph.

11
00:01:05,860 --> 00:01:11,020
We want to begin assessment of the lungs with a big picture view.

12
00:01:11,740 --> 00:01:17,720
We want to assess both the lung volume and density before we start getting into the details

13
00:01:17,720 --> 00:01:19,680
of focal abnormalities within the lung.

14
00:01:20,340 --> 00:01:25,540
Regarding lung volume, you have to be careful when assessing this because sometimes a lung

15
00:01:25,540 --> 00:01:32,680
a poor inspiration can mimic true low lung volumes, and on the other hand, sometimes

16
00:01:32,680 --> 00:01:38,020
somebody who is fit and takes a very good deep breath, that can mimic hyperinflation.

17
00:01:38,420 --> 00:01:42,160
But the first thing you want to look for is, okay, what is the overall lung volume?

18
00:01:42,280 --> 00:01:42,840
Is it normal?

19
00:01:43,440 --> 00:01:44,820
Is it too inflated?

20
00:01:45,140 --> 00:01:47,160
Or are there low lung volumes?

21
00:01:47,700 --> 00:01:50,240
Then you want to look at overall density of the lungs.

22
00:01:50,340 --> 00:01:52,960
Now remember the lungs aren't purely gas.

23
00:01:52,960 --> 00:01:59,000
They do have some density to them because of the bronchi and the pulmonary vessels.

24
00:01:59,140 --> 00:02:04,020
So the bronchovascular markings give the lung its normal radio density.

25
00:02:04,600 --> 00:02:07,520
So when evaluating density, again, you want to say, is this normal?

26
00:02:08,300 --> 00:02:11,060
Or are the lungs too opaque or too lucent?

27
00:02:11,180 --> 00:02:14,920
And what's the pattern of this density if there's an abnormality?

28
00:02:14,920 --> 00:02:18,620
Is this a focal abnormality or a diffuse abnormality?

29
00:02:19,080 --> 00:02:22,260
And then finally, you always want to use symmetry as your friend.

30
00:02:22,720 --> 00:02:27,240
So evaluating the lungs, you want to evaluate both the right and left lungs and compare them

31
00:02:27,240 --> 00:02:28,280
to one another.

32
00:02:28,400 --> 00:02:30,580
So look at symmetry of findings.

33
00:02:32,480 --> 00:02:38,040
Emphysema is a disease process that results in overall increased lung lucency and volume.

34
00:02:38,040 --> 00:02:46,740
The radiographic findings in emphysema are secondary to two processes, lung destruction and hyperinflation.

35
00:02:47,120 --> 00:02:52,780
As we can see in this example, the lungs are overall radiolucent.

36
00:02:53,420 --> 00:02:56,080
There's a relative paucity of pulmonary vessels.

37
00:02:57,180 --> 00:02:59,980
And sometimes you can see thin wall bully.

38
00:03:00,240 --> 00:03:02,960
These are all sequelae of lung destruction.

39
00:03:02,960 --> 00:03:08,780
In addition, you will identify sequelae of hyperinflation.

40
00:03:09,180 --> 00:03:15,640
In this example, we can see flattening of the hemidiaphragms as well as an increase in cranial

41
00:03:15,640 --> 00:03:20,820
caudal height or tall lungs, both sequelae of hyperinflation.

42
00:03:21,880 --> 00:03:29,240
On the lateral view, some of the characteristic findings of emphysema as a reflection of hyperinflation

43
00:03:29,240 --> 00:03:36,460
include, again, flattening of the diaphragm, increased anteroposterior dimension of the

44
00:03:36,460 --> 00:03:41,400
thoracic cavity, and an enlarged retrosternal clear space.

45
00:03:43,610 --> 00:03:50,750
In contrast to emphysema, vibrotic lung disease is characterized by an increased opacity and

46
00:03:50,750 --> 00:03:51,890
diminished lung volumes.

47
00:03:52,390 --> 00:03:57,890
Remember, when assessing overall lung volumes, you want to be careful not to over-recall true

48
00:03:57,890 --> 00:04:02,930
low lung volumes because of a poor inspiratory effort.

49
00:04:04,190 --> 00:04:10,310
In terms of opacity of the lung with vibrotic lung disease, there's an increased opacity secondary

50
00:04:10,310 --> 00:04:12,210
to the thickened interstitium.

51
00:04:12,330 --> 00:04:18,630
So as you can see in this example, assuming appropriate inspiratory effort, the lung volumes

52
00:04:18,630 --> 00:04:27,090
here are low, but there's a diffuse increase in lung opacity from that thickened interstitium.

53
00:04:27,090 --> 00:04:29,390
So this is an example of fibrotic lung disease.

54
00:04:35,780 --> 00:04:39,280
This question tests your knowledge of the radiographic findings of emphysema.

55
00:04:39,600 --> 00:04:45,420
Remember, the two broad categories of findings include lung destruction and hyperinflation.

56
00:04:46,200 --> 00:04:51,400
So lung destruction manifests itself as an irregular radiolucency given inhomogeneous

57
00:04:51,400 --> 00:04:55,820
destruction of the pulmonary vessels and lung parenchyma.

58
00:04:56,220 --> 00:04:58,540
There's a paucity of vessels with thin wall bully.

59
00:04:58,540 --> 00:05:05,480
Hyperinflation manifests itself as flattening of the diaphragms, tall lungs on the frontal

60
00:05:05,480 --> 00:05:08,360
view, so an increased cranial caudal dimension.

61
00:05:09,120 --> 00:05:15,000
And then finally, an increased antroposterior dimension of the thorax that's appreciated on

62
00:05:15,000 --> 00:05:18,000
the lateral view and is the correct answer in this question.

63
00:05:18,000 --> 00:05:26,000
Following assessment for overall lung volume and generalized densities, we then want to compare

64
00:05:26,000 --> 00:05:29,040
the lungs for any focal asymmetries.

65
00:05:29,560 --> 00:05:33,980
One of the things that can cause an asymmetry is a pulmonary opacity.

66
00:05:34,840 --> 00:05:41,000
This refers to any area that preferentially attenuates the x-ray beam and therefore appears

67
00:05:41,000 --> 00:05:43,680
more opaque than the surrounding area.

68
00:05:43,680 --> 00:05:51,180
Now, this is not specific in terms of size or diagnosis and can be caused by multiple etiologies

69
00:05:51,180 --> 00:05:59,060
including airspace disease consolidation, interstitial disease, atelectasis, and nodules or masses.

70
00:05:59,060 --> 00:06:04,860
So in assessing for pulmonary opacities, it's best to compare the lungs right side to left

71
00:06:04,860 --> 00:06:10,460
side as you go from the apex to the base or from base to apex, whatever you prefer.

72
00:06:10,460 --> 00:06:17,740
However, airspace opacification occurs when there is filling of the air spaces with products

73
00:06:17,740 --> 00:06:24,180
of disease, whether it be from fluid, from pulmonary edema, hemorrhage, infection, etc.

74
00:06:24,480 --> 00:06:31,660
This manifests itself radiographically as heterogeneous, poorly marginated, and ill-defined areas of

75
00:06:31,660 --> 00:06:42,660
increased opacity or density as identified on this frontal view of the chest in a patient with diffuse pulmonary edema.

76
00:06:42,660 --> 00:06:50,060
Notice that there's abnormal lung opacity that's fairly symmetric and patchy with ill-defined margins.

77
00:06:50,060 --> 00:06:57,400
Consolidation typically refers to products of disease replacing alveolar air spaces and kind of rendering

78
00:06:57,400 --> 00:07:01,020
the lung solid and more homogeneous in appearance.

79
00:07:02,020 --> 00:07:08,000
Airspace opacification is also characterized by air bronchograms and lack of volume loss

80
00:07:08,000 --> 00:07:14,660
to contrast this with atelectasis, which is characterized by volume loss.

81
00:07:14,660 --> 00:07:23,300
The term air bronchogram refers to a pattern of gas-filled bronchi on a background of airless opaque lung.

82
00:07:23,300 --> 00:07:29,940
So in this example, we can see branching gas-filled structures, which are the airways, that are made visible

83
00:07:29,940 --> 00:07:35,060
by the presence of adjacent airless or opaque lung.

84
00:07:35,060 --> 00:07:43,700
So the presence of air bronchograms implies the patency of the proximal airway as well as evacuation of the alveoli

85
00:07:43,700 --> 00:07:46,420
so they no longer contain air.

86
00:07:46,420 --> 00:07:53,700
So this can be secondary to atelectasis or replacement of the gas within the alveoli by products of disease

87
00:07:53,700 --> 00:07:57,300
such as hemorrhage, edema, or infection.

88
00:07:57,300 --> 00:08:02,660
Therefore, air bronchograms can be present with both atelectasis and airspace disease,

89
00:08:02,660 --> 00:08:09,100
although they are classically described in association with airspace disease on radiography.

90
00:08:10,460 --> 00:08:21,600
In this example, we can identify abnormal patchy opacities with this heterogeneous pattern in the right mid and lower lung.

91
00:08:21,600 --> 00:08:28,480
That becomes especially evident if you are moving from apex to base comparing right and left side.

92
00:08:28,600 --> 00:08:32,720
So compare that area of increased density compared with the left side.

93
00:08:33,340 --> 00:08:35,460
I think the asymmetry becomes more apparent.

94
00:08:36,500 --> 00:08:44,100
In contrast to the previous example, which demonstrated more patchy or heterogeneous airspace opacification,

95
00:08:44,660 --> 00:08:49,700
sometimes the airspace disease can be more confluent or dense in a low bar pattern,

96
00:08:49,700 --> 00:08:54,940
as shown here with left upper lobe consolidation in air bronchograms.

97
00:08:56,140 --> 00:09:01,940
So just take a minute to compare these different patterns of airspace disease.

98
00:09:01,940 --> 00:09:07,940
The example on the left is more of a low bar pattern where there's confluent density in air bronchograms.

99
00:09:08,680 --> 00:09:12,360
Contrast that to the case on the right, which is a lobular pattern,

100
00:09:12,360 --> 00:09:22,540
which gives you that heterogeneous or patchy appearance where you have multiple foci of ill-defined areas of airspace disease.

101
00:09:23,840 --> 00:09:29,480
The silhouette sign refers to the absence of depiction of an anatomic soft tissue border.

102
00:09:30,020 --> 00:09:33,920
That is, a border that's normally identified is no longer identified.

103
00:09:34,800 --> 00:09:40,100
And that's because there's now juxtaposition of structures of similar radiographic attenuation.

104
00:09:40,100 --> 00:09:46,280
So remember on radiography, you identify borders or interfaces between two structures

105
00:09:46,280 --> 00:09:48,820
only when they are of different radiographic density.

106
00:09:49,020 --> 00:09:53,940
So, for example, in this frontal view of the chest, we're able to identify the right heart border

107
00:09:53,940 --> 00:10:02,700
because the fluid and soft tissue density of the heart abuts the primarily gas density of the lung.

108
00:10:02,700 --> 00:10:10,280
But what happens then when the lung adjacent to the heart, which normally is predominantly gas-filled,

109
00:10:10,420 --> 00:10:14,200
is now replaced with products of disease such as pneumonia?

110
00:10:14,680 --> 00:10:20,320
Well, in this example, we've lost the left heart border because of lingular airspace disease.

111
00:10:20,700 --> 00:10:28,020
So now you have the soft tissue density of the heart abutting not the gas density of normal lung,

112
00:10:28,020 --> 00:10:37,740
but instead the soft tissue and fluid density of the lingula, which has been replaced by pneumonia.

113
00:10:40,320 --> 00:10:43,900
Let's use CT to help us understand this concept.

114
00:10:44,320 --> 00:10:48,440
Again, if we look at the right heart border on the radiograph and the CT,

115
00:10:48,820 --> 00:10:53,700
we are able to identify why we can see the right heart border

116
00:10:53,700 --> 00:10:57,020
because we have two different densities abutting one another.

117
00:10:57,320 --> 00:11:05,720
When that normal gas density of the lung is lost, such as in airspace disease from pneumonia in this example,

118
00:11:06,180 --> 00:11:09,360
we are unable now to identify that heart border.

119
00:11:09,920 --> 00:11:14,680
You can see on the CT the disease in the lingula.

120
00:11:15,140 --> 00:11:20,420
So now we have similar radiographic densities abutting one another,

121
00:11:20,420 --> 00:11:22,420
so we lose that left heart border.

122
00:11:23,600 --> 00:11:25,660
So this is an example of the silhouette sign.

123
00:11:27,340 --> 00:11:33,160
Interstitial opacities have a different radiographic appearance than airspace opacities in general.

124
00:11:34,020 --> 00:11:36,220
Often they are described as lines and dots.

125
00:11:36,740 --> 00:11:42,460
So you can have linear or reticular opacifications in addition to dots or tiny nodules.

126
00:11:43,060 --> 00:11:47,520
Now the term reticular refers to this kind of netting appearance.

127
00:11:47,520 --> 00:11:53,100
So it looks like a basketball net or kind of interweaving lines.

128
00:11:53,920 --> 00:11:59,280
Sometimes the pattern is mixed, so both kind of linear and reticular as well as nodular.

129
00:11:59,440 --> 00:12:02,420
And this is termed a reticular nodular pattern.

130
00:12:03,260 --> 00:12:07,380
As an example in this case of a patient with interstitial pulmonary edema,

131
00:12:07,840 --> 00:12:11,300
notice these peripheral lines that are short.

132
00:12:11,700 --> 00:12:14,260
They are horizontally oriented and they touch the pleura.

133
00:12:14,260 --> 00:12:18,780
These are described as curly B lines.

134
00:12:19,480 --> 00:12:24,560
Notice that unlike normal pulmonary vessels, which do not extend all the way to the pleura,

135
00:12:25,180 --> 00:12:30,700
these interstitial lines, which are thickened interstitium from pulmonary edema in this case,

136
00:12:31,000 --> 00:12:32,600
and are referred to as curly B lines,

137
00:12:33,100 --> 00:12:38,100
do actually extend in the peripheral most portion of the lung and contact the pleura.

138
00:12:38,100 --> 00:12:47,520
Here's another example of thickening of the peripheral interstitium secondary to interstitial edema.

139
00:12:48,440 --> 00:12:51,280
As we have a close-up look at that right lung base,

140
00:12:52,120 --> 00:12:56,200
we can identify these horizontal short lines that touch the pleura

141
00:12:56,200 --> 00:13:03,600
that are referred to as the curly's B lines and reflect that thickened peripheral interstitium.

142
00:13:05,740 --> 00:13:10,740
This slide provides a side-by-side comparison of typical airspace and interstitial disease.

143
00:13:10,740 --> 00:13:17,120
The case on the left is airspace disease in that heterogeneous patchy multifocal pattern.

144
00:13:17,120 --> 00:13:21,820
The case on the right is an interstitial pulmonary edema, which we've talked about,

145
00:13:22,200 --> 00:13:27,800
showing that thickened peripheral interstitium, that kind of linear pattern.

146
00:13:28,320 --> 00:13:32,900
Just be mindful that although there are general differences in the radiographic appearance

147
00:13:32,900 --> 00:13:35,600
of interstitial and airspace opacities,

148
00:13:36,140 --> 00:13:43,240
that sometimes in real life they're not always easy to distinguish on radiography and can coexist.

149
00:13:43,240 --> 00:13:51,560
So, for example, in pulmonary edema, you might have both alveolar disease as well as interstitial thickening.

150
00:13:58,340 --> 00:14:03,920
In this example, the abnormality is bilateral increased lung opacity.

151
00:14:04,580 --> 00:14:08,360
And you notice that the opacities are in a sort of a patchy pattern.

152
00:14:09,280 --> 00:14:11,640
Individual opacities are difficult to identify,

153
00:14:11,860 --> 00:14:15,660
so they have ill-defined edges almost as fluffy or cloud-like appearance.

154
00:14:15,880 --> 00:14:18,080
It's characteristic for airspace disease.

155
00:14:18,080 --> 00:14:22,060
We don't see the typical line and dot pattern for interstitial disease.

156
00:14:22,280 --> 00:14:26,520
There's no evidence for hyperinflation or increased lucency,

157
00:14:27,800 --> 00:14:31,200
as we would expect to see with emphysema.

158
00:14:31,380 --> 00:14:35,980
And fibrotic lung disease would be characterized by a sort of coarse interstitial opacities

159
00:14:35,980 --> 00:14:39,320
and low lung volumes, which we don't see in this case.

160
00:14:41,300 --> 00:14:44,760
Atelectasis is another etiology of increased pulmonary opacity.

161
00:14:44,760 --> 00:14:50,200
The key component, or what really defines atelectasis, is volume loss.

162
00:14:51,040 --> 00:14:56,560
The term collapse is typically reserved to describe severe volume loss.

163
00:14:57,240 --> 00:15:01,920
The radiographic appearance of atelectasis will depend on the degree of volume loss,

164
00:15:02,320 --> 00:15:04,640
so how much of the lung is involved,

165
00:15:05,180 --> 00:15:06,360
and the location.

166
00:15:06,860 --> 00:15:11,180
Are we talking right versus left lung, or upper versus lower lobe, for instance?

167
00:15:11,180 --> 00:15:16,660
And then finally, remember that atelectasis, similar to airspace disease,

168
00:15:16,820 --> 00:15:19,080
can be associated with air bronchograms.

169
00:15:20,460 --> 00:15:27,440
In this example, notice the linear or plate-like opacities in both lungs as shown here.

170
00:15:27,440 --> 00:15:34,400
This is characteristic for linear atelectasis, which involves the subsegmental airways.

171
00:15:34,600 --> 00:15:37,580
So this is also called discoid or plate-like atelectasis,

172
00:15:37,580 --> 00:15:40,300
based on this characteristic morphology.

173
00:15:40,880 --> 00:15:43,860
Because this only involves the subsegmental lung,

174
00:15:44,380 --> 00:15:50,580
the degree of volume loss is not typically enough to cause visual shift or diaphragmatic elevation.

175
00:15:50,580 --> 00:15:54,960
Unlike subsegmental atelectasis,

176
00:15:55,400 --> 00:16:02,340
low-bar atelectasis is typically associated with classic signs on radiography of volume loss,

177
00:16:02,420 --> 00:16:08,840
including shift of fissures and elevation of the ipsilateral hemidiaphragm.

178
00:16:09,660 --> 00:16:15,140
The chest radiograph on the left shows a right lower lobe collapse

179
00:16:15,140 --> 00:16:18,960
with shift of the fissure.

180
00:16:18,960 --> 00:16:23,360
Similarly, the case on the right shows a right upper lobe collapse

181
00:16:23,360 --> 00:16:27,660
with cranial displacement of the minor fissure.

182
00:16:27,660 --> 00:16:33,480
So in both of these cases, we see shift of the fissures, indicative of volume loss.

183
00:16:34,160 --> 00:16:38,980
And in both cases, we see elevation of the ipsilateral diaphragm,

184
00:16:39,180 --> 00:16:41,620
another finding of volume loss.

185
00:16:42,860 --> 00:16:45,460
Right upper lobe collapse on a frontal radiograph

186
00:16:45,460 --> 00:16:50,480
is characterized by the upward movement of the minor fissure as shown here.

187
00:16:50,480 --> 00:16:56,360
Notice the interface between the collapsed right upper lobe,

188
00:16:56,620 --> 00:16:59,940
which is now opaque, from the rest of the lung,

189
00:17:00,320 --> 00:17:01,860
which is still well aerated.

190
00:17:02,300 --> 00:17:06,840
And notice the interface between the two is formed by the minor fissure.

191
00:17:07,100 --> 00:17:09,120
Now the shift of the fissure is important

192
00:17:09,120 --> 00:17:12,160
because this helps distinguish a right upper lobe collapse

193
00:17:12,160 --> 00:17:14,040
from a right upper lobe pneumonia.

194
00:17:15,640 --> 00:17:18,680
Middle lobe collapse can be difficult to identify

195
00:17:18,680 --> 00:17:21,260
because the middle lobe is not a large lobe,

196
00:17:21,300 --> 00:17:25,220
and so you may not have the classic findings of volume loss

197
00:17:25,220 --> 00:17:28,520
in terms of ipsilateral diaphragmatic elevation

198
00:17:29,080 --> 00:17:31,800
or contralateral mediastinal shift.

199
00:17:31,860 --> 00:17:35,300
In addition, because of the anatomy of the middle lobe,

200
00:17:35,300 --> 00:17:40,720
it can often be difficult to identify on the frontal view alone.

201
00:17:41,580 --> 00:17:43,500
This is an example of a middle lobe collapse.

202
00:17:43,640 --> 00:17:46,420
Notice the image on the right is a lateral view.

203
00:17:47,220 --> 00:17:51,900
We see a triangular-shaped opacity overlying the heart

204
00:17:51,900 --> 00:17:56,220
that's formed by abnormal opacity from the collapse

205
00:17:56,220 --> 00:17:59,680
with two interfaces that reflect the fissural shift.

206
00:17:59,820 --> 00:18:01,940
So we have a shift of the major fissure

207
00:18:01,940 --> 00:18:07,640
and a shift of the minor fissure resulting in this pie-shaped,

208
00:18:07,780 --> 00:18:10,480
like a slice of a pie or triangular-shaped opacity.

209
00:18:11,020 --> 00:18:13,140
Now, if you think about that morphology

210
00:18:13,140 --> 00:18:15,460
and then you're looking at that from a frontal view,

211
00:18:15,900 --> 00:18:19,080
that can result in really only an ill-defined opacity.

212
00:18:19,440 --> 00:18:21,480
The key in middle lobe collapse

213
00:18:21,480 --> 00:18:23,160
or middle lobe airspace disease

214
00:18:23,160 --> 00:18:27,240
is the identification of the silhouette sign

215
00:18:27,240 --> 00:18:29,720
or the loss of the normal right heart border

216
00:18:29,720 --> 00:18:33,860
from the case of an abnormal middle lobe

217
00:18:33,860 --> 00:18:37,720
from airspace disease or, in this case, collapse.

218
00:18:39,460 --> 00:18:41,200
Right and left lower lobe collapse

219
00:18:41,200 --> 00:18:43,860
have a similar radiographic appearance.

220
00:18:44,220 --> 00:18:47,140
The major fissure rotates backwards and medially

221
00:18:47,140 --> 00:18:51,120
such that the lower lobe lies posterior and medial

222
00:18:51,120 --> 00:18:52,880
in the thoracic cavity.

223
00:18:53,180 --> 00:18:55,360
So this is an example of right lower lobe collapse.

224
00:18:55,360 --> 00:18:59,880
Notice the CT image on the right side

225
00:18:59,880 --> 00:19:03,900
showing the collapsed right lower lobe

226
00:19:03,900 --> 00:19:07,060
with that shift of the major fissure

227
00:19:07,060 --> 00:19:09,380
such that the collapsed right lower lobe

228
00:19:09,380 --> 00:19:12,240
lies posteromedially in the thoracic cavity.

229
00:19:12,740 --> 00:19:14,340
Now, on the chest radiograph,

230
00:19:14,440 --> 00:19:18,280
this manifests itself as this triangular density,

231
00:19:18,520 --> 00:19:20,940
posterior and medial within the thorax,

232
00:19:21,420 --> 00:19:22,440
as shown here.

233
00:19:22,440 --> 00:19:25,500
So you can see that line or interface

234
00:19:25,500 --> 00:19:27,060
created by the fissural shift

235
00:19:27,060 --> 00:19:29,400
that gives this triangular opacity

236
00:19:29,400 --> 00:19:31,320
behind the right heart.

237
00:19:33,240 --> 00:19:36,520
A left lower lobe collapse has a similar morphology

238
00:19:36,520 --> 00:19:38,380
but may be more difficult to identify

239
00:19:38,380 --> 00:19:42,100
because of the presence of the heart on the left side.

240
00:19:42,180 --> 00:19:45,900
But again, it's going to be a shift of the fissure

241
00:19:45,900 --> 00:19:49,480
resulting in this interface behind the heart

242
00:19:49,480 --> 00:19:53,040
and this triangular-shaped opacity characteristic

243
00:19:53,040 --> 00:19:55,460
for lower lobe collapse as shown here.

244
00:19:57,100 --> 00:20:00,220
Because there is no minor fissure on the left,

245
00:20:00,600 --> 00:20:02,380
the appearance of left upper lobe collapse

246
00:20:02,380 --> 00:20:04,760
will differ from that of right upper lobe collapse.

247
00:20:05,560 --> 00:20:06,860
In left upper lobe collapse,

248
00:20:06,860 --> 00:20:09,600
the major fissure shifts anteriorly

249
00:20:09,600 --> 00:20:12,280
as shown on the right-sided image here

250
00:20:12,280 --> 00:20:13,560
which is a lateral view

251
00:20:13,560 --> 00:20:16,140
showing the interface between the opaque

252
00:20:16,140 --> 00:20:21,260
and collapsed left upper lobe with that of the aerated left lower lobe.

253
00:20:21,260 --> 00:20:23,600
So we can see that interface here

254
00:20:23,600 --> 00:20:28,060
as the oblique or major fissure is shifted anteriorly.

255
00:20:28,060 --> 00:20:30,840
Now if we think about how this will look on the frontal view,

256
00:20:30,840 --> 00:20:33,200
you're not going to see the fissure itself

257
00:20:33,200 --> 00:20:35,760
because we're looking at it on FOSS on the frontal view.

258
00:20:35,760 --> 00:20:39,100
Instead, you're just going to see a hazy increased density

259
00:20:39,100 --> 00:20:43,540
of the left lung as we see on the image on the left.

260
00:20:44,220 --> 00:20:47,540
You do see other signs of volume loss in this case

261
00:20:47,540 --> 00:20:51,020
including a mild elevation of the left diaphragm

262
00:20:51,020 --> 00:20:55,420
and sometimes you'll see a ipsilateral mediastinal shift.

263
00:20:55,500 --> 00:20:58,760
In this case, there is slight shift towards the left.

264
00:20:58,760 --> 00:21:01,520
So there are other signs of volume loss

265
00:21:01,520 --> 00:21:04,060
to suggest a lobar collapse.

266
00:21:04,060 --> 00:21:08,720
Finally, with left upper lobe collapse,

267
00:21:08,720 --> 00:21:12,480
the left lower lobe can hyperexpand

268
00:21:12,480 --> 00:21:16,980
such that the superior segment extends over the aortic knob

269
00:21:16,980 --> 00:21:22,240
resulting in this lucency from lower lobe expansion

270
00:21:22,240 --> 00:21:23,120
as shown here.

271
00:21:23,360 --> 00:21:25,480
And this is termed a loop sickle sign.

272
00:21:28,020 --> 00:21:31,300
This is an example from CT of a left upper lobe collapse

273
00:21:31,300 --> 00:21:34,320
to help explain some of those radiographic findings.

274
00:21:34,320 --> 00:21:40,280
Notice the complete opacity of the left upper lobe from collapse.

275
00:21:40,860 --> 00:21:44,680
There's a shift of the major fissure anteriorly

276
00:21:44,680 --> 00:21:46,500
from left upper lobe volume loss.

277
00:21:46,820 --> 00:21:48,280
Compare the fissure shown there

278
00:21:48,280 --> 00:21:51,560
versus the right major fissure

279
00:21:51,560 --> 00:21:53,360
which is more posteriorly located.

280
00:21:53,960 --> 00:21:55,880
Also note the difference in aeration

281
00:21:55,880 --> 00:21:58,820
of the superior segment of the lower lobes.

282
00:21:59,280 --> 00:22:03,780
Here we have a hyper-expanded superior segment on the left

283
00:22:03,780 --> 00:22:06,220
because of the left upper lobe collapse

284
00:22:06,220 --> 00:22:09,580
versus the normal appearance of the superior segment

285
00:22:09,580 --> 00:22:11,140
of the right lower lobe.

286
00:22:13,310 --> 00:22:15,910
The final etiology of abnormal pulmonary opacity

287
00:22:15,910 --> 00:22:17,410
that we are going to discuss

288
00:22:17,410 --> 00:22:19,890
is that of a pulmonary nodule or mass.

289
00:22:20,410 --> 00:22:24,250
In this example, we see multiple pulmonary nodules.

290
00:22:24,250 --> 00:22:26,890
For example, in the right lower lung

291
00:22:26,890 --> 00:22:28,890
as well as in the left mid-lung

292
00:22:28,890 --> 00:22:31,230
and adjacent to the left heart border.

293
00:22:32,230 --> 00:22:35,430
Notice that nodules are rounded opacities

294
00:22:35,430 --> 00:22:39,730
and can be either well-defined or poorly defined.

295
00:22:40,850 --> 00:22:43,650
Just keep in mind when evaluating radiographs

296
00:22:43,650 --> 00:22:47,650
that pulmonary nodules can be very difficult to identify

297
00:22:47,650 --> 00:22:51,150
even if they're quite large depending on their location.

298
00:22:51,150 --> 00:22:53,590
And remember, we discussed the blind spots

299
00:22:53,590 --> 00:22:56,150
on a frontal radiograph

300
00:22:56,150 --> 00:22:57,590
including the lung apices

301
00:22:57,590 --> 00:22:59,430
behind the hyla, behind the heart

302
00:22:59,430 --> 00:23:01,450
and then behind the diaphragm.

303
00:23:02,390 --> 00:23:07,110
In this example, there's a fairly large pulmonary nodule

304
00:23:07,110 --> 00:23:09,550
or mass in the left lung apex

305
00:23:09,550 --> 00:23:10,490
as shown here.

306
00:23:10,910 --> 00:23:13,450
In a blown-up view of this region,

307
00:23:13,570 --> 00:23:15,990
you can identify the nodule a little bit better.

308
00:23:15,990 --> 00:23:20,010
But again, this is very difficult to identify

309
00:23:20,010 --> 00:23:23,410
particularly in those areas that we discussed.

310
00:23:24,510 --> 00:23:27,170
Thus far, I've used the term mass and nodule

311
00:23:27,170 --> 00:23:28,710
almost interchangeably

312
00:23:28,710 --> 00:23:30,670
although there is a definition

313
00:23:30,670 --> 00:23:34,550
that distinguishes the two based on size alone.

314
00:23:34,970 --> 00:23:36,750
So in the example on the left,

315
00:23:37,190 --> 00:23:40,670
there is a opacity in the left superhylar region

316
00:23:40,670 --> 00:23:43,250
shown here that's less than three centimeters

317
00:23:43,250 --> 00:23:46,010
and therefore would be defined strictly

318
00:23:46,010 --> 00:23:47,450
as a pulmonary nodule.

319
00:23:47,890 --> 00:23:49,710
So pulmonary nodules are less than

320
00:23:49,710 --> 00:23:51,310
or equal to three centimeters.

321
00:23:51,990 --> 00:23:54,290
That's in distinction to a pulmonary mass

322
00:23:54,290 --> 00:23:57,150
as shown here in the right lung apex.

323
00:23:57,770 --> 00:24:00,730
Pulmonary masses are greater than three centimeters.

324
00:24:01,170 --> 00:24:03,690
So to distinguish a mass in a nodule,

325
00:24:04,270 --> 00:24:06,170
you need to specifically know

326
00:24:06,170 --> 00:24:08,050
the size of the abnormality.

327
00:24:09,190 --> 00:24:10,910
The pulmonary cavity is defined

328
00:24:10,910 --> 00:24:14,390
as a gas-filled space within a pulmonary nodule or mass

329
00:24:14,390 --> 00:24:17,390
as well as within an area of consolidation.

330
00:24:18,290 --> 00:24:20,650
It's typically produced by expulsion or drainage

331
00:24:20,650 --> 00:24:22,790
of a necrotic portion of the lung

332
00:24:22,790 --> 00:24:24,750
through the bronchial tree

333
00:24:24,750 --> 00:24:27,610
and the cavity wall typically has some thickness

334
00:24:27,610 --> 00:24:30,690
to it to distinguish it from a parenchymal cyst.

335
00:24:31,450 --> 00:24:32,810
So in this example,

336
00:24:32,810 --> 00:24:36,430
there's a right lung cavity with a thick wall

337
00:24:36,430 --> 00:24:43,070
and because there is both a gas and fluid component

338
00:24:43,070 --> 00:24:45,190
in this upright patient on a frontal view,

339
00:24:45,310 --> 00:24:47,750
you're actually able to see a gas fluid level

340
00:24:47,750 --> 00:24:49,630
within the cavity.

341
00:24:50,990 --> 00:24:52,810
We will end this module with the discussion

342
00:24:52,810 --> 00:24:54,430
of the spine sign,

343
00:24:54,550 --> 00:24:56,050
which is a radiographic finding

344
00:24:56,050 --> 00:24:58,250
on the lateral view

345
00:24:58,250 --> 00:25:01,050
that can be caused from abnormal opacity

346
00:25:01,050 --> 00:25:04,210
from any of the etiologies that we've discussed.

347
00:25:04,210 --> 00:25:07,810
So the spine sign refers to an increased opacity

348
00:25:07,810 --> 00:25:10,470
of the lower thoracic spine

349
00:25:10,470 --> 00:25:12,350
and as I just discussed,

350
00:25:12,450 --> 00:25:15,090
can be caused by a number of etiologies.

351
00:25:15,270 --> 00:25:17,470
So remember we talked in prior modules

352
00:25:17,470 --> 00:25:20,770
about the normal gradation of lucency

353
00:25:20,770 --> 00:25:25,130
over the thoracic spine on the lateral view

354
00:25:25,130 --> 00:25:27,270
as we go from lung apex to lung base.

355
00:25:27,730 --> 00:25:28,930
As we go in this direction,

356
00:25:29,030 --> 00:25:30,090
there's more and more lung

357
00:25:30,090 --> 00:25:31,410
and less and less soft tissue

358
00:25:31,410 --> 00:25:34,910
so that as you go from lung apex to base

359
00:25:34,910 --> 00:25:36,330
overlying the spine,

360
00:25:37,050 --> 00:25:39,850
the lung should appear more and more lucent.

361
00:25:40,370 --> 00:25:42,310
The spine sign refers to loss

362
00:25:42,310 --> 00:25:43,490
of this normal gradation.

363
00:25:43,710 --> 00:25:44,990
So in this example,

364
00:25:45,110 --> 00:25:47,330
there's abnormal opacity

365
00:25:47,330 --> 00:25:50,670
overlying the lower thoracic spine.

366
00:25:52,250 --> 00:25:54,910
The spine sign is perhaps best appreciated

367
00:25:54,910 --> 00:25:56,750
using a side-by-side comparison

368
00:25:56,750 --> 00:25:58,790
to a normal lateral chest radiograph,

369
00:25:58,930 --> 00:26:00,830
which in this case is shown on the left image.

370
00:26:01,550 --> 00:26:03,450
Notice that the thoracic spine

371
00:26:03,450 --> 00:26:06,390
becomes less and less opaque

372
00:26:06,390 --> 00:26:07,770
or more and more lucent

373
00:26:07,770 --> 00:26:11,430
as you move from the lung apex

374
00:26:11,430 --> 00:26:13,510
to the lung base as shown here.

375
00:26:13,650 --> 00:26:15,890
So the lower thoracic spine

376
00:26:15,890 --> 00:26:16,930
appears more lucent

377
00:26:16,930 --> 00:26:18,910
than the mid or upper spine.

378
00:26:19,450 --> 00:26:21,310
However, this relationship is lost

379
00:26:21,310 --> 00:26:24,650
in somebody with a positive spine sign.

380
00:26:25,150 --> 00:26:28,330
That is, the lower thoracic spine

381
00:26:28,330 --> 00:26:29,490
appears more opaque

382
00:26:29,490 --> 00:26:30,770
as shown in this case

383
00:26:30,770 --> 00:26:32,150
relative to the mid

384
00:26:32,150 --> 00:26:33,630
and upper thoracic spine

385
00:26:33,630 --> 00:26:35,270
because of the presence

386
00:26:35,270 --> 00:26:37,550
of some abnormal opacity

387
00:26:37,550 --> 00:26:41,030
in the lower lungs.

388
00:26:41,490 --> 00:26:44,710
The key here is simply

389
00:26:44,710 --> 00:26:46,110
to remember to evaluate

390
00:26:46,110 --> 00:26:47,910
for this finding

391
00:26:47,910 --> 00:26:49,450
when you're interpreting

392
00:26:49,450 --> 00:26:51,790
a lateral radiograph of the chest.

393
00:26:51,790 --> 00:26:59,460
This question tests your ability

394
00:26:59,460 --> 00:27:01,580
to identify patterns

395
00:27:01,580 --> 00:27:02,680
of low bar collapse.

396
00:27:02,780 --> 00:27:03,400
In this case,

397
00:27:03,440 --> 00:27:04,340
this is an example

398
00:27:04,340 --> 00:27:06,900
of a left lower lobe collapse.

399
00:27:07,720 --> 00:27:08,420
Remember, the key

400
00:27:08,420 --> 00:27:09,940
for lower lobe collapses

401
00:27:09,940 --> 00:27:11,160
is identification

402
00:27:11,160 --> 00:27:13,720
of these triangular-shaped opacities

403
00:27:13,720 --> 00:27:15,580
along the posterior

404
00:27:15,580 --> 00:27:16,640
and medial aspect

405
00:27:16,640 --> 00:27:18,140
of the thorac.

406
00:27:18,200 --> 00:27:18,920
So in this case,

407
00:27:19,000 --> 00:27:20,180
we see that interface

408
00:27:20,180 --> 00:27:22,560
created by the shift

409
00:27:22,560 --> 00:27:24,680
of the left major fissure

410
00:27:24,680 --> 00:27:26,460
with increased opacity

411
00:27:26,460 --> 00:27:29,980
of the collapsed left lower lobe.

412
00:27:30,340 --> 00:27:31,380
Also in this case,

413
00:27:31,420 --> 00:27:32,320
we have an example

414
00:27:32,320 --> 00:27:34,000
of a bronchial cutoff.

415
00:27:34,500 --> 00:27:35,460
So in our overall

416
00:27:35,460 --> 00:27:36,660
radiographic assessment,

417
00:27:36,800 --> 00:27:37,700
remember we need to look

418
00:27:37,700 --> 00:27:39,640
at the central airways.

419
00:27:40,200 --> 00:27:41,160
And in this example,

420
00:27:41,160 --> 00:27:42,220
we have a cutoff

421
00:27:42,220 --> 00:27:47,500
of the left lower lobe bronchus,

422
00:27:47,500 --> 00:27:48,740
which is another key

423
00:27:48,740 --> 00:27:50,080
to the presence of collapse.

424
00:27:50,080 --> 00:27:52,040
In this module,

425
00:27:52,140 --> 00:27:53,080
we discussed assessment

426
00:27:53,080 --> 00:27:53,780
of the lungs

427
00:27:53,780 --> 00:27:55,740
on the chest radiograph.

428
00:27:56,440 --> 00:27:57,500
Remember, we want to begin

429
00:27:57,500 --> 00:27:58,940
with an overall assessment

430
00:27:58,940 --> 00:28:00,720
of lung volume

431
00:28:00,720 --> 00:28:02,020
and generalized density.

432
00:28:02,600 --> 00:28:04,520
We discussed the differences

433
00:28:04,520 --> 00:28:05,640
between appearance

434
00:28:05,640 --> 00:28:07,060
of pulmonary fibrosis

435
00:28:07,060 --> 00:28:07,800
and emphysema.

436
00:28:08,520 --> 00:28:09,880
We then talked about

437
00:28:09,880 --> 00:28:11,920
the four causes

438
00:28:11,920 --> 00:28:13,520
of pulmonary opacities,

439
00:28:13,640 --> 00:28:15,720
including airspace disease,

440
00:28:15,940 --> 00:28:17,200
interstitial opacities,

441
00:28:17,440 --> 00:28:18,160
atelectasis,

442
00:28:18,160 --> 00:28:19,900
and pulmonary nodules.

443
00:28:21,160 --> 00:28:21,480
Remember,

444
00:28:21,680 --> 00:28:22,400
the appearance

445
00:28:22,400 --> 00:28:24,100
of airspace disease

446
00:28:24,100 --> 00:28:25,380
can be homogeneous

447
00:28:25,380 --> 00:28:26,160
versus patchy.

448
00:28:27,220 --> 00:28:28,020
But in general,

449
00:28:28,660 --> 00:28:29,160
airspace opacities

450
00:28:29,760 --> 00:28:31,180
have ill-defined

451
00:28:31,180 --> 00:28:32,620
kind of fluffy margins

452
00:28:32,620 --> 00:28:34,040
in contrast

453
00:28:34,040 --> 00:28:35,540
to the typical lines

454
00:28:35,540 --> 00:28:36,160
and dots associated

455
00:28:36,800 --> 00:28:38,160
with interstitial disease.

456
00:28:38,840 --> 00:28:39,700
We talked about

457
00:28:39,700 --> 00:28:40,800
the varied appearance

458
00:28:40,800 --> 00:28:41,160
of atelectasis

459
00:28:41,820 --> 00:28:42,740
depending on

460
00:28:42,740 --> 00:28:43,920
the degree

461
00:28:43,920 --> 00:28:45,180
of volume loss

462
00:28:45,180 --> 00:28:46,680
and the location

463
00:28:46,680 --> 00:28:47,100
of this

464
00:28:47,100 --> 00:28:47,700
in terms of

465
00:28:47,700 --> 00:28:48,540
low bar collapse.

466
00:28:48,640 --> 00:28:49,020
But remember,

467
00:28:49,160 --> 00:28:49,980
the key component

468
00:28:49,980 --> 00:28:50,860
with atelectasis

469
00:28:50,860 --> 00:28:52,660
is volume loss.

470
00:28:52,980 --> 00:28:54,640
Sometimes manifests itself

471
00:28:54,640 --> 00:28:56,700
as fissural shift

472
00:28:56,700 --> 00:28:59,780
or diaphragmatic elevation

473
00:28:59,780 --> 00:29:01,640
or mediastinal shift

474
00:29:01,640 --> 00:29:03,780
depending on the degree

475
00:29:03,780 --> 00:29:05,080
of collapse

476
00:29:05,080 --> 00:29:05,920
or atelectasis.

477
00:29:06,480 --> 00:29:07,200
Then finally,

478
00:29:07,340 --> 00:29:08,120
we distinguished

479
00:29:08,120 --> 00:29:09,240
pulmonary nodules

480
00:29:09,240 --> 00:29:10,260
from masses

481
00:29:10,260 --> 00:29:12,600
based on a size cutoff

482
00:29:12,600 --> 00:29:13,500
of three centimeters.

483
00:29:13,500 --> 00:29:15,000
We talked about

484
00:29:15,000 --> 00:29:16,140
pulmonary cavities

485
00:29:16,140 --> 00:29:17,120
which are essentially

486
00:29:17,120 --> 00:29:18,580
gas-filled spaces.

487
00:29:19,440 --> 00:29:20,220
And then we talked

488
00:29:20,220 --> 00:29:21,080
about radiographic

489
00:29:21,080 --> 00:29:21,940
manifestations

490
00:29:21,940 --> 00:29:24,220
of abnormal

491
00:29:24,220 --> 00:29:25,620
pulmonary opacities

492
00:29:25,620 --> 00:29:27,200
including the silhouette

493
00:29:27,200 --> 00:29:28,740
and spine signs.

494
00:29:29,700 --> 00:29:30,440
That brings us

495
00:29:30,440 --> 00:29:31,100
to the end

496
00:29:31,100 --> 00:29:31,760
of this unit.

497
00:29:32,320 --> 00:29:33,020
Please complete

498
00:29:33,020 --> 00:29:33,740
the corresponding

499
00:29:33,740 --> 00:29:34,820
quiz questions

500
00:29:34,820 --> 00:29:35,920
to receive credit

501
00:29:35,920 --> 00:29:36,720
for this course.

